70445162873e-07,-2.3841830625315197e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,70,2075,8,284,856,61,17,481,220,16,16,87,488,220,18,15,4779,323,1077,400,69,2075,15087,387,264,47311,1741,429,198,78045,70,955,2075,593,284,856,61,19,481,220,16,19,87,61,18,488,220,21,17,87,61,17,481,220,24,16,87,488,220,19,17,7110,60,9885,279,2629,315,678,3204,2750,315,400,69,7,16,15,47822,16,15,15,16630,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30,$ and let $f(x)$ be a polynomial such that\n\\[g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42.\\]Find the sum of all possible values of $f(10^{100}).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0972c0e98995acc74c63d8c3ca9dfa01a61cb8dd18ca31657f6114d89c5c423f"},"seconds":66.86444544792175,"seed":1014319670250497837,"stop":"eos","text":"Given the polynomials \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\) and \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we need to determine the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) and then find the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\).\n\nFirst, let's assume that \\( f(x) \\) is a quadratic polynomial since \\( g(f(x)) \\) is a quartic polynomial. Let \\( f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\). Then,\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = (ax^2 + bx + c)^2 - 11(ax^2 + bx + c) + 30.\n\\]\nExpanding this, we get\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = a^2x^4 + 2abx^3 + (2ac + b^2 - 11a)x^2 + (2bc - 11b)x + (c^2 - 11c + 30).\n\\]\nWe need to match this with the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\). By comparing coefficients, we get the system of equations:\n\\[\na^2 = 1, \\quad 2ab = -14, \\quad 2ac + b^2 - 11a = 62, \\quad 2bc - 11b = -91, \\quad c^2 - 11c + 30 = 42.\n\\]\nFrom \\( a^2 = 1 \\), we have \\( a = 1 \\) or \\( a = -1 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( a = 1 \\)**\n\\[\n2b = -14 \\implies b = -7.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = -7 \\) into the next equation:\n\\[\n2c + (-7)^2 - 11 = 62 \\implies 2c + 49 - 11 = 62 \\implies 2c + 38 = 62 \\implies 2c = 24 \\implies c = 12.\n\\]\nWe check the last equation:\n\\[\nc^2 - 11c + 30 = 12^2 - 11 \\cdot 12 + 30 = 144 - 132 + 30 = 42.\n\\]\nThis is satisfied, so \\( f(x) = x^2 - 7x + 12 \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( a = -1 \\)**\n\\[\n2b = 14 \\implies b = 7.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( a = -1 \\) and \\( b = 7 \\) into the next equation:\n\\[\n-2c + 7^2 + 11 = 62 \\implies -2c + 49 + 11 = 62 \\implies -2c + 60 = 62 \\implies -2c = 2 \\implies c = -1.\n\\]\nWe check the last equation:\n\\[\nc^2 - 11c + 30 = (-1)^2 - 11(-1) + 30 = 1 + 11 + 30 = 42.\n\\]\nThis is satisfied, so \\( f(x) = -x^2 + 7x - 1 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\):\n\\[\nf(10^{100}) = (10^{100})^2 - 7 \\cdot 10^{100} + 12 = 10^{200} - 7 \\cdot 10^{100} + 12,\n\\]\n\\[\nf(10^{100}) = -(10^{100})^2 + 7 \\cdot 10^{100} - 1 = -10^{200} + 7 \\cdot 10^{100} - 1.\n\\]\nThe sum of these values is:\n\\[\n(10^{200} - 7 \\cdot 10^{100} + 12) + (-10^{200} + 7 \\cdot 10^{100} - 1) = 11.\n\\]\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{11}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Given the polynomials \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\) and \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we need to determine the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) and then find the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\).\n\nFirst, let's assume that \\( f(x) \\) is a quadratic polynomial since \\( g(f(x)) \\) is a quartic polynomial. Let \\( f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\). Then,\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = (ax^2 + bx + c)^2 - 11(ax^2 + bx + c) + 30.\n\\]\nExpanding this, we get\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = a^2x^4 + 2abx^3 + (2ac + b^2 - 11a)x^2 + (2bc - 11b)x + (c^2 - 11c + 30).\n\\]\nWe need to match this with the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\). By comparing coefficients, we get the system of equations:\n\\[\na^2 = 1, \\quad 2ab = -14, \\quad 2ac + b^2 - 11a = 62, \\quad 2bc - 11b = -91, \\quad c^2 - 11c + 30 = 42.\n\\]\nFrom \\( a^2 = 1 \\), we have \\( a = 1 \\) or \\( a = -1 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( a = 1 \\)**\n\\[\n2b = -14 \\implies b = -7.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = -7 \\) into the next equation:\n\\[\n2c + (-7)^2 - 11 = 62 \\implies 2c + 49 - 11 = 62 \\implies 2c + 38 = 62 \\implies 2c = 24 \\implies c = 12.\n\\]\nWe check the last equation:\n\\[\nc^2 - 11c + 30 = 12^2 - 11 \\cdot 12 + 30 = 144 - 132 + 30 = 42.\n\\]\nThis is satisfied, so \\( f(x) = x^2 - 7x + 12 \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( a = -1 \\)**\n\\[\n2b = 14 \\implies b = 7.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( a = -1 \\) and \\( b = 7 \\) into the next equation:\n\\[\n-2c + 7^2 + 11 = 62 \\implies -2c + 49 + 11 = 62 \\implies -2c + 60 = 62 \\implies -2c = 2 \\implies c = -1.\n\\]\nWe check the last equation:\n\\[\nc^2 - 11c + 30 = (-1)^2 - 11(-1) + 30 = 1 + 11 + 30 = 42.\n\\]\nThis is satisfied, so \\( f(x) = -x^2 + 7x - 1 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\):\n\\[\nf(10^{100}) = (10^{100})^2 - 7 \\cdot 10^{100} + 12 = 10^{200} - 7 \\cdot 10^{100} + 12,\n\\]\n\\[\nf(10^{100}) = -(10^{100})^2 + 7 \\cdot 10^{100} - 1 = -10^{200} + 7 \\cdot 10^{100} - 1.\n\\]\nThe sum of these values is:\n\\[\n(10^{200} - 7 \\cdot 10^{100} + 12) + (-10^{200} + 7 \\cdot 10^{100} - 1) = 11.\n\\]\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:58"}
